UPDATE public.mindcast_live_sessions SET
  session_title          = $s42t_st$Closing the Gap$s42t_st$,
  theme_title            = $s42t_tt$The Courage to Be Seen$s42t_tt$,
  heavy_week_flag        = true,
  s5_source_opening_hook = $s42t_hk$Think of three places you act a bit differently: school, home, close friends, sport, work, online. Some of that is completely normal. Which differences feel chosen and useful, and which feel like exhausting pressure to be accepted?$s42t_hk$,
  s5_source_core_concept = $s42t_cc$Everybody changes how they communicate depending on context. That is social skill, not automatically being fake.
The useful question is: am I choosing how I show up, or am I constantly acting against my own values because I am scared of what people will think?
Being authentic does not mean telling everybody everything.
You are allowed to be private.$s42t_cc$,
  core_concept           = $s42t_cco$Authenticity is not total disclosure. It is acting in ways that are reasonably congruent with your values while choosing what to reveal based on trust, role, safety and context. Privacy is not falseness.
Teen translation: Be more yourself where it is safe without believing you owe people your private life to prove you are authentic.$s42t_cco$,
  teaching_points        = $s42t_tp$1. Different settings get different versions of your behaviour. You may talk differently with a teacher, sibling, close friend and online stranger. That is normal.
2. Privacy is not lying. You can keep personal information, feelings, sexuality, family issues, health information and private experiences to yourself or share them selectively.
3. Honesty can be behavioural. Saying “I don't agree,” “I don't know,” “I don't want to do that,” or “I actually like this” can be authentic without revealing a deep secret.
4. Self-disclosure can help relationships sometimes, but context matters. Research links appropriate disclosure with closeness and liking, but there is no rule that deeper disclosure creates better connection.
5. Trust is built over time. A person being friendly, curious or part of the same group does not automatically earn access to your most private information.
6. Online disclosure is still disclosure. Screenshots, forwarding, public audiences and permanent records can change the risk. “Being real online” is not worth giving away information you want private.
7. Safety comes first. If being open could expose you to bullying, discrimination, family harm or other danger, privacy and trusted-adult support may be the smart choice.
8. You can change. There is no test where you discover your one permanent “real self.” Interests, identity and values can develop.$s42t_tp$,
  signal_metaphor        = $s42t_sm$Being yourself does not mean giving everyone the password to your whole phone. You decide which app, photo or conversation each person gets access to.$s42t_sm$,
  private_write_prompt   = $s42t_pw$Choose one ordinary, low-risk thing you sometimes hide or manage: a music preference, hobby, opinion, reasonable boundary, not knowing something, wanting alone time.
Do not choose sexuality, trauma, family secrets, abuse, mental-health history or anything whose disclosure could affect your safety.$s42t_pw$,
  experiential_exercise  = $s42t_ex$BE MORE REAL WITHOUT OVERSHARING — 18 minutes
For the ordinary situation write:
1. WHAT I REALLY THINK/WANT: …
2. WHAT I USUALLY DO: …
3. WHY I MANAGE IT: fitting in / avoiding conflict / role / privacy / unsure.
4. WHAT IS SAFE TO KEEP PRIVATE: …
5. ONE SMALL HONEST MOVE: …
Examples:
- “Yeah, whatever” → “I'd rather do something else.”
- pretending you know → “I don't get that yet.”
- going along with a joke → “That's not really my thing.”
PRIVACY CIRCLES
Draw four circles:
- PUBLIC: okay for almost anyone;
- FRIENDS: selected people;
- PRIVATE: very few people;
- PROTECTED: not sharing right now.
Put fictional/example information into circles. No real sensitive disclosure required.$s42t_ex$,
  guided_reflection      = $s42t_gr$What part of me am I trying to express?
Can I do that with a small honest behaviour?
What information is mine to keep private?
Has this person earned trust over time?
Am I sharing because I want to or because I think “being brave” means oversharing?$s42t_gr$,
  journaling_prompt      = $s42t_jp$Complete:
I can be more myself by . I do not need to tell people . One privacy boundary I want to keep is .$s42t_jp$,
  intention_prompt       = $s42t_ip$Make one low-risk honest move this week. It can be a preference, boundary, disagreement or “I don't know.”$s42t_ip$,
  core_affirmation       = $s42t_ca$I can be honest and still be private. I decide what I share, who gets access, and when being more myself is safe.$s42t_ca$,
  weekly_practice_mon    = $s42t_pm$Say one ordinary preference honestly.$s42t_pm$,
  weekly_practice_wed    = $s42t_pw2$Practise one “no,” “not yet,” or “I need time.”$s42t_pw2$,
  weekly_practice_fri    = $s42t_pf$Review one online privacy choice.$s42t_pf$,
  weekly_practice_sun    = $s42t_ps$Bring back: what helped you feel more congruent without oversharing?$s42t_ps$,
  facilitator_notes      = $s42t_fn$## Aim
Correct the previous unsafe premise that genuine connection requires teens to reveal hidden truths.
## Run the room
- Never ask teens to reveal “something true you've never told anyone.”
- Do not invite public vulnerability or encourage private disclosures to peers.
- No “Real Me” cards containing personal information.
- If a teen is weighing disclosure of identity, abuse, mental health or family conflict, do not coach the disclosure; use safeguarding and appropriate trusted-adult/professional support.
## Why this week exists — the evidence
Self-disclosure research shows associations between appropriate disclosure and interpersonal liking/closeness, but the effects are contextual and moderated. Research on online disclosure also shows that audience and medium can change relational outcomes.
The evidence does not justify telling adolescents that “genuine connection is only possible when people see the real you.”
## We deliberately do not claim
- fitting in and belonging are scientifically separable in a simple way;
- vulnerability guarantees closeness;
- being private is fear;
- online authenticity requires disclosure;
- teens should close the gap between private and public selves in unsafe settings.
## Source trail
Collins & Miller (1994) disclosure/liking meta-analysis · context-sensitive online disclosure research.$s42t_fn$,
  updated_at = now()
