UPDATE public.mindcast_live_sessions SET
  session_title          = $s47t_st$Compound Interest for a Person$s47t_st$,
  theme_title            = $s47t_tt$The Long Game — Persistence With Feedback$s47t_tt$,
  heavy_week_flag        = false,
  s5_source_opening_hook = $s47t_hk$Think of something you improved at because you kept practising. Now think of something where repeating the same thing did not help much. What was different?$s47t_hk$,
  s5_source_core_concept = $s47t_cc$Persistence matters. So does knowing what to persist with.
The old “1% better every day” and compound-interest idea sounds motivating, but humans do not improve at a fixed daily rate.
A better long-game loop is:
TRY → CHECK → ADJUST → TRY AGAIN → REVIEW WHETHER THE GOAL STILL FITS.$s47t_cc$,
  core_concept           = $s47t_cco$Persistence is useful when the goal still matters, the method is workable, and feedback is used to adjust the approach. Repetition does not compound at a fixed rate, hidden progress cannot be assumed, and changing strategy or stopping can be rational.
Teen translation: Keep going when there is a reason to keep going — but check the method, collect feedback and change direction when the evidence says you should.$s47t_cco$,
  teaching_points        = $s47t_tp$1. Progress can be uneven. Skills often improve in bursts, plateaus and messy periods. A bad day does not erase learning, but it also does not prove hidden progress is happening.
2. Practice works better when it contains feedback. If you keep doing the same wrong technique, you may simply get more fluent at the mistake.
3. Grit got oversold. Research finds perseverance is related to achievement, but “grit” does not explain success nearly as strongly or independently as popular talks sometimes imply.
4. The 1%-better-every-day line is arithmetic, not psychology. People do not compound like bank accounts.
5. Track what you can control before the final result appears. Training sessions, pages written, questions attempted, applications sent, practice problems corrected — choose an indicator that actually relates to the goal.
6. Change the method when the data says to. Ask a coach, teacher or knowledgeable person what you are practising incorrectly or inefficiently.
7. Stopping is sometimes smart. If you no longer want the goal, the cost is too high, or the method is not working after reasonable adjustment, quitting can free time for something better.
8. Do not persist through injury, unsafe situations or serious exhaustion to prove commitment. Health and safety are information too.$s47t_tp$,
  signal_metaphor        = $s47t_sm$A long game is like ranking up with patch notes. You keep playing, but when the game changes or your strategy stops working, you update the build.$s47t_sm$,
  private_write_prompt   = $s47t_pw$Choose one long-term goal that is safe and genuinely yours. Write what you are currently doing repeatedly and what feedback you have about whether it works.$s47t_pw$,
  experiential_exercise  = $s47t_ex$LONG-GAME DEBUG — 18 minutes
Complete:
1. GOAL: …
2. WHY IT STILL MATTERS: …
3. WHAT I REPEAT: …
4. WHAT I TRACK: one behaviour or skill measure.
5. WHO/WHAT GIVES FEEDBACK: …
6. ONE ADJUSTMENT TO TEST: …
7. REVIEW DATE: …
8. STOP/PAUSE RULE: what evidence would justify changing direction?
Then turn it into a seven- or fourteen-day experiment rather than a multi-year promise.
SUNK-COST SCENARIO
Use fictional examples: a sport someone no longer likes, an expensive course that is poor quality, a friendship/activity maintained only because of years invested. Ask: If you had not already invested anything, would you choose it today?$s47t_ex$,
  guided_reflection      = $s47t_gr$Do I still want this goal?
Am I practising the right thing?
What feedback am I getting?
What could I change rather than just trying harder?
What would make stopping or pausing a strong decision?$s47t_gr$,
  journaling_prompt      = $s47t_jp$Complete:
For the next  days, I will practise , track , ask  for feedback, and review whether to continue or change on .$s47t_jp$,
  intention_prompt       = $s47t_ip$Run one evidence-based short experiment instead of promising yourself you will “never quit.”$s47t_ip$,
  core_affirmation       = $s47t_ca$I can persist and still change my mind. I use feedback, adjust the method, and keep going only when the direction still makes sense.$s47t_ca$,
  weekly_practice_mon    = $s47t_pm$Track one useful indicator.$s47t_pm$,
  weekly_practice_wed    = $s47t_pw2$Ask for one specific piece of feedback.$s47t_pw2$,
  weekly_practice_fri    = $s47t_pf$Change one part of the method if needed.$s47t_pf$,
  weekly_practice_sun    = $s47t_ps$Bring back: continue, adjust, pause or stop?$s47t_ps$,
  facilitator_notes      = $s47t_fn$## Aim
Remove the old compound-interest/1% misinformation and “grit = success” framing.
## Run the room
- No three-year letters as commitments to a future identity.
- Do not praise “never quitting.”
- Avoid weight, diet, appearance and exercise-through-pain examples.
- Encourage appropriate adult/coaching input for skill or academic feedback.
## Why this week exists — the evidence
Meta-analytic work on grit finds perseverance has some association with performance but that the broader grit construct is not a powerful unique predictor once related traits are considered. Practice research supports repetition with targeted feedback, while the amount of variance explained by deliberate practice differs greatly by domain.
This supports adaptive persistence, not blind perseverance.
## We deliberately do not claim
- 1% daily improvement compounds;
- grit is a major predictor of life success;
- roots are growing whenever results are invisible;
- persistence automatically builds character;
- quitting is weakness.
## Source trail
Credé et al. (2017) grit meta-analysis · Macnamara et al. deliberate-practice meta-analytic work · sunk-cost research.$s47t_fn$,
