  s5_source_opening_hook = $s51c_hk$If you learn to tie your shoes, do you need a shoe-tying lesson every Sunday forever? No. You use the skill when shoes need tying. Some Mindcast tools can work like that too.$s51c_hk$,
  s5_source_core_concept = $s51c_cc$The year is nearly finished.
Children do not need to promise to grow forever, practise every day or keep coming to the programme.
They can take a few useful ideas with them and use them when they fit.
The pattern is:
REMEMBER → USE IF HELPFUL → ASK FOR HELP IF BIG → PUT IT AWAY WHEN DONE.$s51c_cc$,
  core_concept           = $s51c_cco$A useful skill does not need to become a lifelong daily practice. Children can remember a few tools, use them when helpful, forget them for a while, ask adults for help, and move on from the programme without needing to keep “working on themselves.”
Child translation: Choose a few tools you like. Keep them in your toolbox. You do not have to use them every day or keep coming to Mindcast for them to belong to you.$s51c_cco$,
  teaching_points        = $s51c_tp$1. You do not need every tool. Different children choose different things.
2. A tool can stay in the toolbox until you need it. You do not need to practise kind self-talk or rest menus every day just because you learned them.
3. Forgetting is okay. If you remember a tool months later, you can simply use it again.
4. Some tools stop being useful. You can grow out of an activity or find a better way.
5. Big problems need grown-ups. A Mindcast tool is not enough for danger, abuse, serious illness, bullying or very big feelings that need adult/professional support.
6. You do not need Mindcast to own the tool. The words can change, the picture can get lost, and the idea can still belong to you.
7. You are allowed to just be a kid. Play, rest, learn, be bored, make mistakes and enjoy things without always trying to improve yourself.$s51c_tp$,
  signal_metaphor        = $s51c_sm$A toolbox can sit in the cupboard. It does not get sad when you are outside playing.$s51c_sm$,
  private_write_prompt   = $s51c_pw$Draw:
- one tool I want to keep;
- one tool I might use later;
- one thing from the year I am happy to leave behind.$s51c_pw$,
  experiential_exercise  = $s51c_ex$MY TAKE-HOME TOOLBOX — 15 minutes
For each chosen tool draw/write:
1. USE IT WHEN: …
2. DO THIS: …
3. ASK A GROWN-UP WHEN: …
4. I CAN STOP USING IT WHEN: it is not useful / I do not need it / I found another way.
Add a big label:
THE TOOLS ARE MINE. I DO NOT HAVE TO KEEP COMING HERE TO USE THEM.
GROWN-UP ROUTE
Practise sorting scenarios into:
- MY TOOL MIGHT HELP
- TELL/ASK A GROWN-UP
- BOTH
This prevents the toolbox becoming a self-help solution for every problem.$s51c_ex$,
  guided_reflection      = $s51c_gr$Which tool do you actually want to keep?
When would you use it?
When would you ask a grown-up instead?
What are you happy to stop practising?
Can you remember that finishing Mindcast is okay?$s51c_gr$,
  journaling_prompt      = $s51c_jp$Finish:
I am taking  with me because it helps when . I can leave  behind. My tools belong to me.$s51c_jp$,
  intention_prompt       = $s51c_ip$Use a tool only if a real situation needs it this week.$s51c_ip$,
  core_affirmation       = $s51c_ca$I can keep the tools that help me and leave the rest. I do not have to practise every day, and finishing this programme is okay.$s51c_ca$,
  weekly_practice_mon    = $s51c_pm$Put your toolbox somewhere safe.$s51c_pm$,
  weekly_practice_wed    = $s51c_pw2$Notice whether you needed any tool at all. Not needing one is okay.$s51c_pw2$,
  weekly_practice_fri    = $s51c_pf$Tell a caregiver one tool you want them to remind you about only if you ask.$s51c_pf$,
  weekly_practice_sun    = $s51c_ps$Bring back: what are you taking with you?$s51c_ps$,
  facilitator_notes      = $s51c_fn$## Aim
Prepare children for a clean ending and ownership outside Mindcast.
## Run the room
- Remove “you never finish growing,” “adventure continues forever,” and permanent daily-practice assignments.
- Do not ask children to make a “next chapter” commitment tied to Mindcast ideas.
- Do not display toolboxes as evidence of programme identity unless participation is optional and non-sensitive.
- Caregivers should not enforce ongoing practices because Mindcast assigned them.
## Why this week exists — the evidence
Learning transfer is demonstrated when a skill can be used outside the teaching context. Maintenance is stronger when tools are simple, relevant and supported by normal environments, but not every learned skill requires ongoing rehearsal.
For children, ending structured instruction cleanly is itself important: the programme should not imply that continued participation is needed to remain okay or continue developing.
## Parent
Ask:
- “Which tool do you want to keep?”
- “Do you want me to remind you, or only if you ask?”
- “Is this a tool problem or a grown-up problem?”
Do not create a daily Mindcast routine unless the child independently wants one and it remains low-pressure.
## We deliberately do not claim
- children never finish growing and therefore should keep practising;
- one tool should be kept for life;
- the programme is an ongoing adventure they need to continue;
- ending structure risks losing progress.
## Source trail
Learning-transfer and maintenance principles translated developmentally; safeguarding and anti-dependency design govern the ending.$s51c_fn$,
