  facilitator_notes      = $s52c_fn$## Aim
Make the final child session warm, clear and non-dependent.
## Run the room
- Remove “MAKE IT UNFORGETTABLE,” “this is what it was all building toward,” hero-return and “adventure continues” language.
- No hands-in-the-middle collective phrase, vows, letters read aloud or compulsory peer appreciation.
- No child is told the year created courage, transformation or a “growing true self.”
- Photos/celebration food/decoration, if used, follow normal policy and are just a normal end-of-programme celebration — not a sacred or initiatory ceremony.
- Do not promote continued child attendance during the closing activity.
## Why this week exists — the evidence
Mindcast has not directly established that its full 52-week child curriculum causes psychological transformation. The final session therefore uses memory retrieval, choice, clear transition and support handoff rather than programme-level impact claims.
For children especially, healthy closure should make the end understandable and preserve ordinary attachments to caregivers, school and community rather than elevating the programme relationship.
## Parent
Tell caregivers explicitly:
- the 52-week curriculum is complete;
- no Mindcast homework continues;
- use tools only if the child finds them helpful;
- do not treat continued attendance as necessary for maintaining progress;
- ordinary family, school, healthcare and community supports remain primary.
## We deliberately do not claim
- every child changed over the year;
- the group is a family/community that should continue indefinitely;
- an ending is really a beginning;
- children should make an ongoing growth commitment;
- ceremony or group witnessing consolidates psychological change.
## Source trail
Individual-week evidence trails · learning retrieval/transfer principles · safeguarding and anti-dependency design for programme closure.$s52c_fn$,
